# Lore, part 5: fuller entries that replace the short ones of parts 2 to 4 (first half).
LORE = {
'kamba': ("The Kamba of the dry plateau east of Nairobi were the great long-distance traders of nineteenth-century East Africa, carrying ivory to the coast and guiding caravans inland; under the British many served in the army and police.",
          "They are celebrated woodcarvers whose figures sell around the world, keep the kilumi dance of rain and healing, eat isyo of maize and beans, and are known in Kenya for their humour and their skill with bows and with business."),
'nande': ("The Nande of North Kivu, in the green hills under the Rwenzori mountains, built a famous trading network in the twentieth century that reached Dubai and China, even through decades of war in eastern Congo.",
          "They farm bananas, coffee and potatoes on volcanic soil, keep their mwami chiefs and clan rites, and the markets of Butembo and Beni are among the busiest of Central Africa."),
'kalenjin': ("The Kalenjin are Nilotic herders and farmers of the western Rift Valley highlands of Kenya; Nandi warriors resisted the Uganda railway for a decade around 1900, and Kalenjin politicians have shaped modern Kenya.",
             "Born at altitude and raised on mursik, sour milk in smoked gourds, and ugali, they have produced many of the greatest distance runners in history; circumcision and age-set ceremonies still mark the passage to adulthood."),
'edo': ("The Edo built the Kingdom of Benin, a forest state with a walled capital, famous city plans and the Oba's court; its bronze and ivory art was looted by a British expedition in 1897 and became one of the great causes of returned heritage.",
        "Their Oba still reigns in Benin City and his festivals, Igue above all, bring the city out in coral beads and white wrappers; the guilds of bronze casters on Igun Street work as they did five hundred years ago."),
'garo_khasi': ("The Garo and Khasi are the peoples of the Meghalaya hills, the abode of the clouds; the Khasi chiefdoms resisted the British in the Anglo-Khasi war of 1829 to 1833, and both peoples later became largely Christian.",
               "Both are matrilineal: children take the mother's clan name and the youngest daughter inherits the house. The Khasi grow living bridges from the roots of rubber trees across the gorges of the wettest place on Earth, and the Garo dance the Wangala at harvest to the beat of long drums."),
'fon': ("The Fon kingdom of Dahomey, founded in the seventeenth century around Abomey, grew rich and feared through war and the slave trade; its regiments of women soldiers, the Agojie, fought the French until the kingdom fell in 1894.",
        "Vodun was born in their land and crossed the Atlantic to Haiti and Brazil; they still honour it with drums, dances and the festival of 10 January, and the bas-reliefs of the royal palaces of Abomey tell the history of their kings."),
'luhya': ("The Luhya are a Bantu people of more than a dozen subgroups in the green, crowded farmland of western Kenya, around Kakamega and Bungoma, and the second-largest people of the country.",
          "They love bullfighting between village champions, dance the fast isukuti to drums, eat chicken and ugali at every celebration, and their Kakamega forest is the last remnant of the Guinea-Congo rainforest in Kenya."),
'nupe': ("The Nupe kingdom of the middle Niger, founded according to legend by Tsoede in the fifteenth century, became part of the Fulani emirates after the jihad of the nineteenth century, with its capital at Bida.",
         "Bida is famous for its craft guilds: glass bead makers, brass and silver smiths and weavers of fine cloth; the Nupe hold durbars on horseback and their masquerades and river fishing mark the year."),
'nuba': ("The Nuba are more than fifty peoples speaking dozens of languages in the Nuba mountains of South Kordofan; they took refuge there from slave raiders for centuries and suffered terribly in the Sudanese civil wars.",
         "Their wrestling matches, with fighters painted in ash, are famous, as are their body paintings and scarification; they farm sorghum on terraced hillsides and live in villages of round houses."),
'chaga': ("The Chaga and Pare farm the fertile southern slopes of Kilimanjaro and the Pare mountains; the Chaga chiefdoms built irrigation channels centuries ago and formed one of Africa's first coffee cooperatives in the 1920s.",
          "They live in the kihamba, a home garden of bananas, coffee and vegetables under shade trees, brew mbege banana beer for every celebration, and are famous in Tanzania for business, education and Christmas trips home to the mountain."),
'guna_embera': ("The Guna of Panama's San Blas islands rose against the Panamanian police in the Tule Revolution of 1925 and won one of the first indigenous autonomies of the Americas; the Embera live along the rivers of the Darien.",
                "Guna women sew the famous molas, layered appliqued panels, and wear beaded wini bands on arms and legs; their congress meets every evening. The Embera paint themselves with black jagua dye and weave fine baskets of palm."),
'ijaw': ("The Ijaw are the people of the Niger Delta creeks, one of the oldest peoples of southern Nigeria; their city-states of Bonny, Nembe, Kalabari and Okrika traded slaves and later palm oil with Europe.",
         "They are fishers and canoe people who hold boat regattas and water masquerades with dancers on canoes; Owu masks, pepper soup with fresh fish, and the ancient Egbesu tradition of warriors belong to them."),
'ibibio': ("The Ibibio and Efik are peoples of the Cross River coast; the Efik city of Calabar was a great trading port for slaves and then palm oil, and its leaders signed treaties with the British in the nineteenth century.",
           "Their kitchens are thought the finest in Nigeria: edikang ikong, afang and fresh fish. The Ekpe leopard society once governed Calabar, its masquerades still dance, and the Calabar Carnival in December is the largest street party in West Africa."),
'frisian': ("The Frisians have lived along the North Sea coast since Roman times, building terps, artificial mounds, against the floods; their Frisian freedom meant no feudal lords, and their language is the closest living relative of English.",
            "They skate the Elfstedentocht, the eleven-cities tour, whenever the canals freeze hard enough, play kaatsen and fierljeppen, pole-vaulting across ditches, and keep their language and black-and-white cattle."),
'sara': ("The Sara are the largest people of southern Chad, farmers of the savannah who suffered from slave raids by the Sahelian sultanates before French rule; Chad's first president came from among them.",
         "Their yondo initiation of young men, held in the bush, is a secret and lasting rite; they grow millet, sorghum and cotton, and their music and dance accompany every season of farming."),
'fur': ("The Fur sultanate ruled Darfur from the seventeenth century until the British killed its last sultan, Ali Dinar, in 1916; the war of 2003 brought massacres and the flight of millions.",
        "They are farmers of the Jebel Marra mountains and the plains around them, growing millet, oranges and tobacco; Fur villages are known for their round thatched houses and their weekly markets."),
'soninke': ("The Soninke founded the empire of Ghana, Wagadu, the first of the great West African empires, which grew rich on gold and salt more than a thousand years ago; its fall scattered them across the Sahel.",
            "They are traders and travellers: Soninke villages send their sons to France and beyond and build schools, mosques and wells with the money. Their griots still sing the legend of Wagadu and the serpent Bida."),
'ossetian': ("The Ossetians are an Iranian people of the central Caucasus, heirs of the Alans, a steppe people of the ancient world; they became mostly Orthodox Christians and were divided between North and South Ossetia.",
             "They bake three round Ossetian pies for every feast, keep the Nart sagas of ancient heroes, honour sacred groves and shrines, and are proud of their wrestlers and dancers."),
'ilocano': ("The Ilocanos of the narrow coast of northern Luzon rose against Spain under Diego Silang in 1762; poor land made them the Philippines' great emigrants, to Hawaii, California and the rest of the country.",
            "They are known for thrift and hard work, for pinakbet and bagnet, for the inabel woven blankets and for the baroque churches of Paoay and Vigan, an old Spanish town of stone streets."),
'wayuu': ("The Wayuu of the Guajira peninsula between Colombia and Venezuela were never conquered by Spain; they acquired horses and cattle and traded with English and Dutch smugglers.",
          "Their society is organised in matrilineal clans, their palabreros settle disputes by word, not force, and their women weave the bright mochila bags; the yonna dance, with women in red, is held for feasts and coming-of-age."),
'mari': ("The Mari are a Finnic people of the middle Volga; they were conquered by Moscow in the sixteenth century and resisted for decades, and many kept their old religion through the Soviet era.",
         "They are among the last people of Europe with a living pagan faith: they pray in sacred groves with priests called kart, and they embroider white dresses and play the gusli and bagpipes at festivals."),
'bemba': ("The Bemba kingdom, under the Chitimukulu, dominated the plateau of north-eastern Zambia in the nineteenth century; later its young men worked the copper mines and their language became the lingua franca of the Copperbelt.",
          "They cut and burn the bush to grow millet in the chitemene system, keep the Ukusefya pa Ng'wena ceremony of their kings, and eat nshima with every meal."),
'udmurt': ("The Udmurts are a Finnic people of the Kama region who came under Moscow in the sixteenth century; many kept their traditions despite conversion and Soviet industry.",
           "They keep folk faith and ancestral rites alongside Orthodoxy, embroider in red, bake perepech pies, and their village grandmothers, the Buranovo Babushki, sang at Eurovision."),
'akha_lisu': ("The Akha and Lisu are hill peoples who moved south from Yunnan through Burma, Laos and Thailand over two centuries, living in villages on the highest ridges.",
              "Akha women wear tall headdresses of silver and beads, every village has its ceremonial gate and a great swing for the harvest festival, and the Lisu are known for bright clothing and new year dances."),
'cham': ("The Cham kingdom of Champa ruled the coast of central Vietnam for over a thousand years, trading in aloes and cinnamon, until Vietnamese expansion absorbed it by the nineteenth century.",
         "Their brick towers at My Son and Po Nagar are among the wonders of Southeast Asia; some Cham keep a Hindu-derived faith and others Islam, and they celebrate the Kate festival and weave and make pottery."),
'karen': ("The Karen are a people of the hills between Burma and Thailand; many became Christian under Baptist missionaries in the nineteenth century, and they have fought for autonomy since 1949.",
          "They weave bright cloth, keep the wrist-tying ceremony to call back wandering souls, celebrate their new year with bronze frog drums and the don dance, and many live as refugees along the Thai border."),
'serer': ("The Serer of Senegal had the kingdoms of Sine and Saloum and kept their own religion longer than their neighbours; the poet-president Leopold Sedar Senghor was a Serer.",
          "They wrestle in the laamb, Senegal's national sport, keep the Xooy divination ceremony of their seers, and grow millet and peanuts on the plateaus of Sine."),
'swazi': ("The Swazi kingdom was built by Sobhuza I and Mswati II in the nineteenth century, survived colonial rule, and remains one of the world's last absolute monarchies, renamed Eswatini.",
          "They hold Incwala, the kingship ceremony of the first fruits, and Umhlanga, the reed dance where young women bring reeds to the queen mother; colourful mahiya cloths and shields mark these feasts."),
'qeqchi': ("The Q'eqchi' Maya of Alta Verapaz were brought peacefully into the Church by Bartolome de las Casas; their lands later became coffee plantations owned by German settlers.",
           "They keep the Maya calendar with spiritual guides, grow maize, cardamom and coffee, and their cave and mountain shrines are still visited with candles and copal incense."),
'dinka_nuer': ("The Dinka and Nuer are the largest peoples of South Sudan, cattle herders of the vast Sudd swamps; they fought in the long civil wars for southern independence, won in 2011, and then against each other.",
               "A man's favourite ox gives him his song name; they move between cattle camps by the river and villages in the wet season, mark manhood with forehead scars, and are among the tallest people on Earth."),
'mordvin': ("The Mordvins, Erzya and Moksha, are Finnic peoples of the middle Volga, absorbed by Moscow from the fifteenth century; they spread widely across Russia as farmers.",
            "They embroider their dresses with dense red and black patterns, keep the Rasken Ozks festival of the Erzya, and the sculptor Stepan Erzia took his name from them."),
'madurese': ("The Madurese of the dry island of Madura have long migrated to eastern Java for work; their princes served the Dutch, and they are among the most devout Muslims of Indonesia.",
             "Their bull races, the karapan sapi, are run on decorated pairs of bulls; they are famous as traders and for sate Madura with peanut sauce, and their speech is known for its frankness."),
'salar_dongxiang': ("The Dongxiang, Salar and Bonan are small Muslim peoples of Gansu and Qinghai; the Salar came from Samarkand in the fourteenth century, by legend with a white camel and a Quran.",
                    "They keep mosques and Sufi orders, speak Mongolic and Turkic languages among Chinese neighbours, and the Bonan are famous for their knives."),
'rohingya': ("The Rohingya are Muslims of northern Rakhine with roots going back centuries; denied citizenship in Burma since 1982, most were driven to Bangladesh in 2017.",
             "They keep their language, written in a script of their own, their Islamic schools and their customs in the camps and abroad, and dream of returning to their villages."),
'hawaiian': ("The Native Hawaiians are Polynesian navigators who reached the islands perhaps a thousand years ago; Kamehameha united them in 1810, and the Kingdom of Hawaii was overthrown by American businessmen in 1893.",
             "They dance the hula and chant the oli, surf, which they invented, eat poi from taro, hold the luau, and revived the voyaging canoe Hokule'a, which sailed around the world."),
'crimean_tatar': ("The Crimean Tatars ruled the Crimean Khanate under the Giray dynasty until Russia annexed it in 1783; Stalin deported the whole people to Central Asia in 1944, and they returned only after 1989.",
                  "They eat chiburek and yantyk, sing and dance the haytarma, and remember the deportation every 18 May."),
'samoan_tongan': ("The Samoans and Tongans are Polynesian peoples; the Tongan kingdom was never colonised, and Samoa was the first Pacific nation to become independent, in 1962.",
                  "They live by fa'a Samoa and the Tongan way: chiefs and family councils, kava ceremonies, tapa cloth, tattoos, Sunday church and the umu earth oven."),
'dan': ("The Dan and Kru are peoples of the forests of Liberia and Ivory Coast; the Kru were famed sailors who worked on European ships along the coast for centuries.",
        "The Dan are famous for their masks, used for justice, entertainment and to call spirits, and their stilt dancers; the Kru keep songs and dances from their seafaring history."),
'sukuma': ("The Sukuma and Nyamwezi are the largest peoples of Tanzania; the Nyamwezi were the great caravan traders of the interior under chiefs like Mirambo.",
           "Sukuma dance societies compete with snakes and porcupines in their acts, and their villages farm cotton and herd cattle south of Lake Victoria."),
'iban': ("The Iban are the largest people of Sarawak, once feared headhunters who moved across Borneo; the White Rajahs of Sarawak recruited them as rangers.",
         "They live in longhouses along the rivers, celebrate Gawai Dayak at harvest, weave pua kumbu blankets with spirit patterns, and wear tattoos that tell the story of their journeys."),
'komi': ("The Komi are a Finnic people of the northern Urals who became Christian through St Stephen of Perm, who created a script for them in the fourteenth century.",
         "They hunt, fish and herd reindeer in the north, keep wooden villages along the rivers, and their traditions of the forest and the sacred birch are still alive."),
'kayapo_xavante': ("The Kayapo and Xavante are Ge peoples of central Brazil; the Kayapo leaders Raoni and others fought to protect their lands from dams and ranchers.",
                   "They paint their bodies in black and red geometric patterns, run log relay races, wear feather headdresses, and hold great ceremonies of naming."),
'ganda': ("The Kingdom of Buganda, ruled by the Kabaka from Mengo on the shores of Lake Victoria, was the most powerful state of East Africa in the nineteenth century and gave its name to Uganda.",
          "They eat matoke, steamed bananas, make bark cloth from fig trees, dance bakisimba to royal drums, and the kingdom's clans each keep a totem."),
'igorot': ("The Igorot of the Cordillera of Luzon were never conquered by Spain; their ancestors carved the rice terraces of Banaue and the Ifugao two thousand years ago.",
           "They hold canao feasts, weave striped cloth, bury their dead in hanging coffins in Sagada, and keep their mountain agriculture."),
'cherokee': ("The Cherokee and Muscogee nations of the south-east built towns and constitutions; they were forced west along the Trail of Tears in the 1830s.",
             "Sequoyah created the Cherokee syllabary in 1821, and they keep stomp dances, the stickball game, and green corn ceremonies."),
'bashkir': ("The Bashkirs are a Turkic people of the southern Urals who joined Russia in the sixteenth century and rose many times, with Salavat Yulayev in Pugachev's rebellion.",
            "They keep honey of the wild bees, kumis and horse breeding, play the kurai flute, and hold Sabantuy in summer."),
'basotho': ("The Basotho were united by Moshoeshoe in the mountains of Lesotho in the nineteenth century, who defended them against the Boers and placed them under British protection.",
            "They wear blankets and the conical mokorotlo hat, ride Basotho ponies in the high mountains, and sing famoso songs."),
'yolngu': ("The Yolngu of north-eastern Arnhem Land traded with Makassar sailors for centuries; their bark petitions of 1963 began the land rights movement.",
           "They play the yidaki, paint bark with sacred clan designs, and keep their Garma festival and songlines."),
'slovene': ("The Slovenes lived for a thousand years under the Habsburgs and kept their language through the Protestant writers; Slovenia became independent in 1991.",
            "They are famous beekeepers, bake potica, ski and climb Triglav, and gather for wine and song."),
'tzotzil': ("The Tzotzil and Tzeltal Maya of the Chiapas highlands rose in rebellions against colonial rule and in the Zapatista rising of 1994.",
            "In San Juan Chamula they worship in a church filled with pine needles and candles; they weave and hold carnival with Maya and Catholic rites."),
'degar': ("The Degar or Montagnards are the highland peoples of Vietnam's Central Highlands, Jarai, Rhade, Bahnar and others, who fought with the Americans in the Vietnam war.",
          "They play gong ensembles, live in longhouses, and drink rice wine from jars through long straws."),
'kpelle': ("The Kpelle are the largest people of Liberia, farmers of upland rice; their Poro and Sande societies train young people in the forest.",
           "They play the triangle-frame zither and the slit drum, and tell stories with music, the meni-pele."),
'yucatec': ("The Yucatec Maya built Chichen Itza, Uxmal and Mayapan; they rose in the Caste War of 1847, one of the longest indigenous revolts in the Americas.",
            "They speak Maya, cook cochinita pibil in earth ovens, live in thatched houses, and dance the jarana in white huipiles."),
'newar': ("The Newars built the cities of the Kathmandu valley under the Malla kings and gave Nepal its art and architecture; the Gorkhas conquered them in 1768.",
          "They keep the living goddess Kumari, the Indra Jatra and chariot festivals, and their feasts, samay baji and yomari, are famous."),
'acholi': ("The Acholi and Lango are Nilotic peoples of northern Uganda, who suffered greatly in the war of the Lord's Resistance Army.",
           "They dance the bwola, a royal dance, and larakaraka, the courtship dance, and their reconciliation ceremony mato oput has healed wartime wounds."),
'tay_nung': ("The Tay and Nung are Tai peoples of northern Vietnam, rice farmers of the mountain valleys, who took part in the anti-French resistance.",
             "They play the dan tinh lute and sing then songs, wear indigo clothes, and live in stilt houses."),
'chuvash': ("The Chuvash are the only surviving speakers of the Oghur branch of Turkic, heirs of Volga Bulgaria; they became Orthodox under Russian rule.",
            "They embroider in red, wear coin headdresses, celebrate Akatuy, and brew beer for all feasts."),
'dagomba': ("The Dagomba kingdom of northern Ghana, founded in the fifteenth century, is ruled by the Ya Na from Yendi.",
            "Their drummers recite the kings' history, they celebrate the Fire Festival and Damba, and eat tuo zaafi."),
'breton': ("The Bretons came from Britain in the fifth and sixth centuries; Brittany was an independent duchy until it joined France in 1532.",
           "They keep pardon pilgrimages, the fest-noz night dances, the bombard and biniou pipes, crepes and cider."),
'kachin': ("The Kachin are a people of the hills of northern Burma, largely Baptist Christians, who have fought for autonomy since the 1960s.",
           "They hold the Manau festival, dancing in great lines around painted posts, and are known for jade mining."),
'taiwanese_ind': ("The Taiwanese Austronesians are the original peoples of Taiwan, Amis, Atayal, Paiwan and others, from whom the Austronesian expansion across the Pacific may have begun.",
                  "They hold harvest festivals with song and dance, weave, tattoo, and their languages are among the most diverse of the family."),
'tiv': ("The Tiv are farmers of the Benue valley in Nigeria, famous among anthropologists for their society without chiefs.",
        "They dance the swange, make the striped black-and-white anger cloth, and are known as yam farmers."),
'turkana': ("The Turkana and Karamojong are Nilotic herders of the dry north-west of Kenya and Uganda, who resisted British control.",
            "They wear bead necklaces and mud-packed headdresses, sing songs to their bulls, and raid cattle."),
'afar': ("The Afar live in the Danakil depression, one of the hottest places on Earth, with sultanates of Aussa and Tadjoura.",
         "They herd camels and goats and mine salt in caravans to the highlands."),
'luo': ("The Luo are Nilotic people who migrated from the Nile to Lake Victoria; Barack Obama's father was one of them.",
        "They fish the lake, play benga music and the nyatiti lyre, and are known for their politics and their funerals."),
'dai': ("The Dai of Xishuangbanna are a Tai people whose kingdom of Sipsongpanna was ruled by its own princes until 1950.",
        "They are Theravada Buddhists who celebrate the water-splashing festival, dance the peacock dance, and live in bamboo stilt houses."),
'pedi': ("The Pedi kingdom of the Maroteng dynasty resisted the Boers and British under Sekhukhune in the nineteenth century.",
         "They play the kiba reed pipes, and their initiation schools and beer brewing continue."),
'toraja': ("The Toraja of Sulawesi kept their own religion, Aluk To Dolo, in the mountains.",
           "They hold great funerals with buffalo sacrifices, keep their dead in cliff graves guarded by tau-tau effigies, and build tongkonan houses with boat-shaped roofs."),
'bai_naxi': ("The Bai built the kingdom of Dali, and the Naxi of Lijiang developed the Dongba pictographic script.",
             "The Bai serve three-course tea, and the Naxi play an orchestra of ancient music."),
'dayak': ("The Dayak are the native peoples of Borneo, once headhunters, who lived in longhouses along the rivers.",
          "They hold the Gawai harvest, weave, carve and tattoo."),
'moro': ("The Moro peoples of Mindanao and Sulu had sultanates that resisted Spain for three centuries and later the Americans.",
         "They dance the singkil, weave the malong, and build boats called vinta with colourful sails."),
'torres': ("The Torres Strait Islanders are Melanesians of the islands between Australia and New Guinea; Eddie Mabo won native title in 1992.",
           "They dance with dhari headdresses and sing island songs."),
}
